  "transcript": "# Trade in a turbulent world: how should the UK deploy its trade instruments?\n\n## Trade is volatile but manufacturers are resilient\n> Despite geopolitics and tariffs, UK goods exporters have held up thanks to adaptability, though growth is uneven and costly.\n\nChair: Maxwell, thanks for joining us. We want practical examples of how volatility affects manufacturers—customs blockages, digital certification, things that hit firms selling things like spectacles.\nLord Anderson of Swansea: After WWII there seemed to be more consensus on trade. Now protectionism looks stronger. Do you see any signs of hope?\nMaxwell Green: There has been real instability—post‑EU adjustment, Middle East conflicts, US tariffs, Iran tensions—but manufacturers are resilient. Exports fell only about 5% in the first half of this year despite big shocks.\nMaxwell Green: The picture is volatile by market: US exports to us dropped a lot, China and some European hubs rose. Imports into the UK from many countries also increased sharply.\nMaxwell Green: Our survey shows more UK manufacturers exporting (up 8%) and continued positive export output. But overall manufacturing output has slowed and firms are bearing costs from adapting supply chains and energy price shocks.\n\n\n## Which sectors and deals have helped\n> Binding FTAs give long‑term certainty; recent US and India arrangements helped autos, steel and other sectors, while fast deals like Australia gave quick wins.\n\nBaroness Verma: Which sectors have done well, and where are current policies deterring development?\nMaxwell Green: Sectors linked to big market deals have held up. Automotive and steel benefited from the US arrangement; aluminium and steel exports to the US rose after that agreement.\nMaxwell Green: Industrial strategy priority sectors are expected to grow, and FTA access is crucial for them.\nBaroness Lawlor: You said manufacturers prefer binding agreements. What non‑binding or quicker arrangements have worked?\nMaxwell Green: Binding FTAs are best for certainty and investment, but non‑binding understandings and sector deals can help in fast‑moving markets. Australia was negotiated quickly and was useful; India took four‑and‑a‑half years but is already cutting tariffs on many goods.\nMaxwell Green: CPTPP opens fast‑growing Asian markets and will keep expanding. Sector‑specific deals—like on critical minerals or energy—can be used when full FTAs stall for political reasons.\nChair: We noted goods exports to Australia have been flat or slightly down since that deal—why might that be?\nMaxwell Green: Overall figures look flat but some sectors improved. Implementation takes time; imports from Australia have risen and feed into UK manufacturing for re‑export. We need better implementation to realise the full gains.\n\n\n## Barriers after a deal: awareness, guidance and finance\n> Signing an agreement is only the start: businesses need earlier, clearer, sector‑specific guidance, easier access to export finance, and help using deals in practice.\n\nLord Stevenson of Balmacara: What should trade policy aim to do differently to benefit industry?\nMaxwell Green: Market access matters, but firms must know how to use it. Improve FTA utilisation through government‑industry collaboration, and provide guidance earlier—months before a deal comes into force.\nMaxwell Green: Access to finance is a major barrier; we support expanding UK Export Finance's lending capacity so exporters can invest and scale up.\nMaxwell Green: Domestic policy matters too: high industrial energy costs—around 46% higher than peers—harm competitiveness. Some levies are charged through electricity bills; funding these by general taxation would reduce manufacturers' costs.\nMaxwell Green: Also consider a review of import tariffs to make inputs cheaper, boosting export competitiveness.\n\n\n## Practical improvements: implementation and market guides\n> Make UK wants simpler, sector‑targeted market guides, better promotion of emerging sectors, clearer routes under multilateral vs bilateral deals, and earlier Government outreach.\n\nBaroness Verma: Is the lag in guidance caused by poor coordination across Whitehall?\nMaxwell Green: The main issue is awareness and timing, not necessarily coordination. Sector‑specific market access guides—pulling in DBIST and Defra where needed—would help firms act from day one.\nLord German: Many firms may not follow the WTO closely. What practical value does it offer your members?\nMaxwell Green: The WTO provides shared rules that create predictability. Even if it looks slow publicly, its norms and behind‑the‑scenes work help stabilise expectations across markets, which manufacturers value.\nLord German: So the WTO's rule‑setting is useful even if not visible day‑to‑day?\nMaxwell Green: Yes. More transparency about its activity would help industry appreciate its role, but the core benefit is consistent rules and predictability.\nLord Boateng: Are there international best practices we should copy to help SMEs export?\nMaxwell Green: Australia promotes its FTA very well with a clear, user‑friendly website. The EU has detailed online market‑by‑market guides showing consumption and opportunities. Canada separates bilateral vs CPTPP benefits clearly—these model practical, navigable guidance systems.\nMaxwell Green: The UK has an 'ask a question' DBIST function, but firms need ready‑made market guides and lists of emerging sector opportunities, not just established trade flows.\nMaxwell Green: For niche producers like cheesemakers, a simple market guide showing tariff rules, certification steps, and the best route (bilateral or multilateral) would make a big difference.\n\n\n## Conclusions and follow‑up\n> Make UK urges viewing deal signing as the start of implementation, improving guidance and finance access, tackling energy costs, and promoting opportunities early to help SMEs and manufacturers use trade tools.\n\nChair: Any final points? Please send follow‑up material; the committee would welcome more detail.\nMaxwell Green: Thanks. Key messages: FTAs give certainty and should be followed by early, sector‑specific guidance; increase export finance capacity; review import tariffs and energy charging; learn from other countries' market promotion.\nChair: Thank you, Maxwell. That was very helpful and practical. We'll look out for your further notes.\n",
